 published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Nebraska remains one of the most reliably Republican states in presidential politics, with a partisan lean of roughly ten points toward the GOP. The 2028 race is open because the incumbent president is term-limited, which removes any incumbency advantage and leaves the contest resting on the state's underlying partisan balance.\n\nNo Nebraska-specific presidential polls, expert ratings, or prediction-market prices have been published yet. Recent coverage centers on the competitive Senate race between Ricketts and Osborn and on congressional contests, including the Omaha-area district that has split its ticket, but none of that directly changes the statewide presidential picture. The Republican nominee is strongly favored to carry Nebraska, with meaningful uncertainty because the national environment for 2028 is still unknown.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1258, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1632, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1797, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1802, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.1712, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1741, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.1653, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1776, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1721, "margin": -10.0}]}